+## Split native RISC-V CI after cancellation
+
+Native [job 119677151197](https://github.com/wago-org/wago/actions/runs/39734486778/job/119677151197)
+starts at 06:33:40 UTC and is cancelled at 08:04:01 UTC. That is approximately
+90 minutes, matching the configured job limit. All prior steps, including the
+public ownership/compiler-free matrix, pass. In the guard step, ordinary Wago
+thread checks and the next runtime guard checks pass before cancellation. The
+runner then kills an active Go compiler. There is no reported failed test or
+Go test timeout. The job limit, rather than a demonstrated runtime defect,
+explains this cancellation.
+
+Split native qualification into two matrix jobs. `runtime` runs smoke, CLI and
+standalone, compiler/runtime correctness, and guards/threads. `ownership` runs
+the complete public ownership and compiler-free matrix on its own runner.
+That matrix retains its temporary artifact directory and prepare/load order.
+Both jobs keep the same 90-minute limit, two-core Go settings, serialized
+compiler functions, exact RISE runner label, fixture manifests, and fork gate.
+Disable matrix fail-fast so a failure in one group does not cancel the other.
+Every original command runs exactly once across the two jobs. No test, timeout,
+assertion, or production code changes.
+
+Actionlint 1.7.7 validates the workflow: 1.937 seconds including build, peak RSS
+200,952 KiB. A coverage check verifies all five original commands, exact group
+selection, the original time limit, unchanged setup/environment/fixture bindings,
+and no matrix fail-fast. Documentation passes for 85 Markdown files in
+0.061 seconds, with peak RSS 27,316 KiB. Native qualification of this split is
+pending. The
+new jobs duplicate runner setup and cold builds; reduced elapsed job time is
+expected from the split, but native total cost and timing remain unmeasured.
+
+The PR stays draft and unmerged. Its known unused-table and A-to-B-to-A scalar
+relay root defects remain unresolved. A CI scheduling fix does not qualify
+that GC root design or justify merging it.
